  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThis Morning through the Weekend...\n\nIt is another clear and calm morning across the region as the \ninfluence of broad sfc high pressure and upper level ridging \ncontinues to bring quiescent weather across much of the eastern \nCONUS. Typical ridge-valley and urban-rural differences in \ntemperatures are observed, with readings ranging from the upper 30s \nto the low 50s at this hour. The main thing we're watching through \nmid morning will be the development of patchy fog in valleys and \nnear bodies of water where dewpoint depressions will be lowest. \nPatchy frost also can't be ruled out in the coldest spots; however, \ncoverage should be limited so no headlines are anticipated.\n\nOutside of patchy valley fog development, no significant weather is \nexpected this weekend. Pleasant fall weather is expected to continue \nas high pressure east of the Appalachians and upper ridging over the \nMississippi Valley continue to exert their influence, bringing mild \nand dry conditions to central KY and southern IN. As the upper ridge \ngets nudged slightly eastward through the weekend, marginal rises in \n1000-850 mb thicknesses should support temperatures trending a \ndegree or so above persistence each day, with highs generally in the \nlow-to-mid 70s. Large diurnal temperature swings should also \ncontinue, with temps dropping into the 40s in most areas each night.\n\nNext Week through the end of October...\n\nBlocking high pressure over the Gulf of Alaska will keep mean \nsynoptic-scale troughing over the western CONUS through early next \nweek, with upper ridging and sfc high pressure continuing to support \ndry weather across the region. A few shortwave disturbances may try \nto eject along the top of the eastern US ridge; however, they should \nbe fairly moisture-starved and would be expected to bring little \nmore than a few extra clouds during the mid-week period. As a \nresult, dry weather is expected to persist through all of next week, \nwith recent global model guidance trending downward for rain chances \nlate next week. Temperatures should be relatively consistent from \nday-to-day next week, and should be above average overall.\n\nThis extended stretch of rain-free weather will lead to increasing \nprecipitation deficits for both the month of October and \nmeteorological fall (since Sep 1st). Worsening drought conditions \nare likely where they currently exist, and the spatial extent of \ndrought will likely increase over the coming 7-10 days.\n\nThe next chance of rain on the horizon arrives Halloween weekend, \nthough only about 20% of GEFS/EPS solutions show rain on Halloween. \nBetter chances for rain should hold off until the first few days of \nNovember, when a stronger system may move across the Ohio Valley \n(forecast confidence medium, stay tuned for more details next week).",
